==Old English==

===Etymology===
From Germanic ''*waroþō'', an extension of a stem-verb ''*wāro-'' ‘be on guard’ (OE [[warian]]).  Cognate with Old High German [[warta]] ‘watch’ (German [[Warte]] ‘watchtower’), Old Saxon [[warda]] (Middle Dutch [[warde]]), Old Norse [[varða]] ‘cairn’.[(Vardia)] guarding in Bulgarian

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/wæɑrd/}}

===Noun===
'''weard''' {{m}} (''plural:'' '''[[weardas]]''') {{f}} (''plural:'' '''[[wearda]]''' or '''[[wearde]]''')

# {{i|masculine only}} [[guardian]], [[watchman]]
# {{i|feminine only}} [[guard]], the action of looking out for danger

